WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.116 --> 00:00:02.426
Hello and welcome back to J D Science.

2
00:00:02.543 --> 00:00:06.258
In today's G C S E Chemistry lesson, we are going to

3
00:00:06.339 --> 00:00:09.985
explore chromatography, an important separation technique used to

4
00:00:10.066 --> 00:00:13.421
identify and separate substances within mixtures.

5
00:00:13.816 --> 00:00:17.508
We will examine how paper chromatography works, explain why

6
00:00:17.566 --> 00:00:21.176
different substances separate, and learn how to calculate

7
00:00:21.211 --> 00:00:22.407
R F values.

8
00:00:22.744 --> 00:00:24.880
Let us begin with a familiar example.

9
00:00:25.158 --> 00:00:29.303
Imagine writing on a piece of paper using a black felt-tip pen.

10
00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:33.286
Although the ink appears black, it may contain several different

11
00:00:33.332 --> 00:00:34.238
coloured dyes.

12
00:00:34.691 --> 00:00:37.872
How can we find out whether the ink contains one dye or

13
00:00:37.918 --> 00:00:39.010
a mixture of dyes?

14
00:00:39.532 --> 00:00:41.958
One method is paper chromatography.

15
00:00:42.353 --> 00:00:46.452
Chromatography separates substances according to how they distribute

16
00:00:46.487 --> 00:00:48.391
themselves between two phases.

17
00:00:48.843 --> 00:00:52.210
These are called the stationary phase and the mobile phase.

18
00:00:52.547 --> 00:00:56.355
In G C S E paper chromatography, the stationary

19
00:00:56.413 --> 00:01:00.326
phase is commonly described as the chromatography paper, while

20
00:01:00.349 --> 00:01:03.344
the mobile phase is the solvent moving through the paper.

21
00:01:03.797 --> 00:01:08.139
More precisely, separation often depends on how substances

22
00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:12.214
interact with the paper and the water associated with its cellulose

23
00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:14.954
fibres, as well as the moving solvent.

24
00:01:15.476 --> 00:01:18.356
Let us examine how the experiment works.

25
00:01:18.693 --> 00:01:22.315
First, draw a pencil line near the bottom of a strip of

26
00:01:22.408 --> 00:01:23.778
chromatography paper.

27
00:01:23.952 --> 00:01:25.531
This is called the baseline.

28
00:01:25.705 --> 00:01:29.490
We use pencil rather than ink because graphite does not

29
00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:33.774
normally dissolve in the solvent and interfere with the results.

30
00:01:34.053 --> 00:01:37.954
Next, place a small spot of the ink sample on the baseline.

31
00:01:38.198 --> 00:01:39.637
Allow the spot to dry.

32
00:01:39.846 --> 00:01:43.666
Then place the paper inside a container containing a small amount

33
00:01:43.736 --> 00:01:45.094
of suitable solvent.

34
00:01:45.431 --> 00:01:48.727
The solvent level must remain below the baseline.

35
00:01:49.064 --> 00:01:50.399
Why is this important?

36
00:01:50.794 --> 00:01:54.649
If the ink spot is submerged in the solvent, the sample

37
00:01:54.695 --> 00:01:58.666
may dissolve directly into the liquid rather than travelling up the paper.

38
00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:02.973
The solvent gradually moves up the paper by capillary action.

39
00:02:03.182 --> 00:02:06.654
As it moves, it carries the dissolved dyes with it.

40
00:02:06.897 --> 00:02:10.752
Different dyes travel different distances because they have different

41
00:02:10.798 --> 00:02:14.165
affinities for the mobile and stationary phases.

42
00:02:14.444 --> 00:02:18.461
A dye that is more strongly attracted to the mobile phase relative

43
00:02:18.508 --> 00:02:21.898
to the stationary phase generally travels farther.

44
00:02:22.235 --> 00:02:26.112
A dye that is more strongly retained by the stationary phase travels

45
00:02:26.159 --> 00:02:27.355
a shorter distance.

46
00:02:27.877 --> 00:02:31.581
As a result, the different dyes separate into individual

47
00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:32.347
spots.

48
00:02:32.742 --> 00:02:36.387
When the solvent has travelled an appropriate distance, remove

49
00:02:36.422 --> 00:02:40.149
the paper and immediately mark the position of the solvent front

50
00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:41.182
with a pencil.

51
00:02:41.298 --> 00:02:42.901
Allow the paper to dry.

52
00:02:43.423 --> 00:02:47.127
The resulting pattern of spots is called a chromatogram.

53
00:02:47.951 --> 00:02:51.643
Now, let us consider how chromatography can help identify

54
00:02:51.712 --> 00:02:52.583
substances.

55
00:02:52.792 --> 00:02:56.484
If a sample produces one spot under a particular set of

56
00:02:56.542 --> 00:03:00.536
conditions, this suggests that it may contain only one component

57
00:03:00.594 --> 00:03:02.243
detectable by that method.

58
00:03:02.486 --> 00:03:06.480
If it produces several spots, it contains several components.

59
00:03:06.817 --> 00:03:10.497
However, one spot does not conclusively prove that

60
00:03:10.544 --> 00:03:14.224
a substance is pure because different substances can sometimes

61
00:03:14.282 --> 00:03:15.954
travel the same distance.

62
00:03:16.198 --> 00:03:19.193
Now, let us examine R F values.

63
00:03:19.646 --> 00:03:22.200
R F stands for retention factor.

64
00:03:22.375 --> 00:03:26.322
The R F value is calculated using the following formula:

65
00:03:26.659 --> 00:03:30.467
R F equals the distance travelled by the substance divided

66
00:03:30.525 --> 00:03:33.207
by the distance travelled by the solvent front.

67
00:03:33.543 --> 00:03:36.771
Both distances must be measured from the baseline.

68
00:03:37.049 --> 00:03:40.764
The distance travelled by a substance is measured to the centre of

69
00:03:40.834 --> 00:03:41.542
its spot.

70
00:03:41.879 --> 00:03:43.504
Let us work through an example.

71
00:03:43.713 --> 00:03:46.233
A student carries out paper chromatography.

72
00:03:46.511 --> 00:03:49.646
One dye travels six centimetres from the baseline.

73
00:03:49.890 --> 00:03:53.617
The solvent front travels eight centimetres from the same baseline.

74
00:03:53.896 --> 00:03:55.893
Calculate the R F value.

75
00:03:56.171 --> 00:03:57.669
We begin with the formula.

76
00:03:57.843 --> 00:04:01.825
R F equals distance travelled by the substance divided by distance

77
00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:03.497
travelled by the solvent front.

78
00:04:03.892 --> 00:04:07.166
Substituting the values gives six divided by eight.

79
00:04:07.341 --> 00:04:11.218
Six divided by eight equals zero point seven five.

80
00:04:11.507 --> 00:04:15.547
Therefore, the R F value is zero point seven five.

81
00:04:15.826 --> 00:04:19.506
Notice that R F values have no units because we are

82
00:04:19.541 --> 00:04:23.187
dividing one distance by another distance measured in the same

83
00:04:23.256 --> 00:04:23.825
units.

84
00:04:24.162 --> 00:04:27.819
For ordinary paper chromatography, R F values

85
00:04:28.028 --> 00:04:30.768
normally lie between zero and one.

86
00:04:31.163 --> 00:04:33.648
Now, let us try another example.

87
00:04:33.891 --> 00:04:37.897
A dye travels three point five centimetres, while the solvent

88
00:04:37.967 --> 00:04:40.509
front travels seven centimetres.

89
00:04:40.718 --> 00:04:42.622
What is the R F value?

90
00:04:42.831 --> 00:04:45.420
Pause for a moment and calculate your answer.

91
00:04:45.594 --> 00:04:48.137
The correct answer is zero point five.

92
00:04:48.381 --> 00:04:51.956
We obtain this by dividing three point five by seven.

93
00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:54.069
Well done if you got that correct.

94
00:04:54.464 --> 00:04:58.168
An important point for your G C S E examination

95
00:04:58.319 --> 00:05:02.812
is that R F values should only be compared meaningfully

96
00:05:03.149 --> 00:05:06.144
when the chromatography conditions are the same.

97
00:05:06.597 --> 00:05:10.417
Changing the solvent, paper or experimental conditions

98
00:05:10.568 --> 00:05:12.901
can change the R F value.

99
00:05:13.354 --> 00:05:17.603
Now, let us tackle a G C S E examination question.

100
00:05:18.056 --> 00:05:22.747
Explain why two dyes in a mixture separate during paper chromatography.

101
00:05:23.199 --> 00:05:25.475
This question is worth three marks.

102
00:05:25.719 --> 00:05:30.061
A model answer would be: The two dyes have different solubilities

103
00:05:30.189 --> 00:05:34.322
in the mobile phase and different attractions to the stationary phase.

104
00:05:34.566 --> 00:05:38.351
As the solvent moves through the paper, the dyes are carried

105
00:05:38.409 --> 00:05:39.605
at different rates.

106
00:05:39.814 --> 00:05:43.796
Consequently, they travel different distances and separate

107
00:05:43.970 --> 00:05:45.700
into individual spots.

108
00:05:45.909 --> 00:05:49.601
Notice that this answer explains the separation in terms

109
00:05:49.659 --> 00:05:53.421
of both phases, rather than simply saying that one dye

110
00:05:53.502 --> 00:05:55.185
moves faster than the other.

111
00:05:55.394 --> 00:05:59.411
That is the level of scientific explanation required for a strong

112
00:05:59.527 --> 00:06:01.397
G C S E answer.

113
00:06:01.548 --> 00:06:05.669
Before we finish, remember that chromatography is used to separate

114
00:06:05.728 --> 00:06:08.758
and help identify substances in mixtures.

115
00:06:09.094 --> 00:06:12.763
The mobile phase moves through the stationary phase, carrying

116
00:06:12.844 --> 00:06:14.853
substances at different rates.

117
00:06:15.097 --> 00:06:18.742
The R F value is calculated by dividing the distance

118
00:06:18.777 --> 00:06:22.771
travelled by a substance by the distance travelled by the solvent front.

119
00:06:22.945 --> 00:06:26.707
If you found this explanation helpful, please subscribe to J D

120
00:06:26.858 --> 00:06:30.480
Science and visit j d science dot co dot u k

121
00:06:30.933 --> 00:06:34.811
for more G C S E Chemistry lessons, calculations

122
00:06:34.869 --> 00:06:36.459
and examination practice.

123
00:06:36.738 --> 00:06:39.605
Thank you for watching, and I will see you in the next

124
00:06:39.675 --> 00:06:40.128
lesson.
